1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,065
Why does this little town have the

2
00:00:02,065 --> 00:00:03,584
craziest border on Earth?

3
00:00:03,934 --> 00:00:06,364
On the border between Belgium and the

4
00:00:06,364 --> 00:00:08,794
Netherlands sits a town split in two:

5
00:00:08,794 --> 00:00:12,538
Baarle-Hertog, which is Belgian, and Baarle-Nassau, which

6
00:00:12,538 --> 00:00:13,129
is Dutch.

7
00:00:13,479 --> 00:00:16,845
Baarle-Hertog is made of twenty-two separate pieces

8
00:00:16,845 --> 00:00:19,815
of Belgium, scattered inside the Netherlands.

9
00:00:20,165 --> 00:00:23,034
And inside some of those Belgian pieces

10
00:00:23,034 --> 00:00:26,197
are even smaller pieces of the Netherlands.

11
00:00:26,197 --> 00:00:28,037
Enclaves inside enclaves.

12
00:00:28,387 --> 00:00:30,572
The borders are marked right on the

13
00:00:30,572 --> 00:00:33,381
streets, with crosses and lines, running past

14
00:00:33,381 --> 00:00:35,192
shops, cafés and front doors.

15
00:00:35,542 --> 00:00:38,619
Some houses are split between both countries.

16
00:00:38,619 --> 00:00:41,285
One even has the border running through

17
00:00:41,285 --> 00:00:43,268
its front door, so it has two

18
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:44,225
house numbers.

19
00:00:44,575 --> 00:00:48,207
Why? Medieval land deals between the Lords

20
00:00:48,207 --> 00:00:51,148
of Breda and the Dukes of Brabant,

21
00:00:51,148 --> 00:00:55,040
finally confirmed by the Treaty of Maastricht

22
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:55,732
in 1843.

23
00:00:56,082 --> 00:00:58,784
Today, about three thousand people live in

24
00:00:58,784 --> 00:01:01,035
the Belgian part. For some of them,

25
00:01:01,035 --> 00:01:03,029
crossing a border is as easy as

26
00:01:03,029 --> 00:01:04,509
walking to the kitchen.

27
00:01:04,859 --> 00:01:06,992
If your house were here, which country

28
00:01:06,992 --> 00:01:08,846
would your bedroom be in? Tell me

29
00:01:08,846 --> 00:01:09,744
in the comments.

